WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.113
The bar turns, and a small, hard plate moves along it.

2
00:00:05.663 --> 00:00:08.275
Its cutting edge peels a chip off the steel.

3
00:00:08.825 --> 00:00:11.170
That plate is called an insert.

4
00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.692
It sits in a tool holder: a steel body with a clamp, which fits into the turret.

5
00:00:18.592 --> 00:00:23.018
The edge gets hot, so the tool must stay hard when it is hot.

6
00:00:23.568 --> 00:00:24.032
Worn?

7
00:00:24.582 --> 00:00:27.461
Then you turn the insert to the next edge.

8
00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:35.690
HSS is high-speed steel: tough, cheap and easy to grind, for small drills and taps.

9
00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:41.410
Cemented carbide is grains of tungsten carbide, bound with cobalt.

10
00:00:41.961 --> 00:00:47.373
It stays hard when it gets hot, and cuts about three to four times faster.

11
00:00:47.924 --> 00:00:49.120
But it is brittle.

12
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.495
Almost every carbide tool has a coating:

13
00:00:52.495 --> 00:00:55.465
a layer a few thousandths of a millimetre thick,

14
00:00:55.665 --> 00:00:57.337
against heat and wear.

15
00:00:58.537 --> 00:01:04.214
The box carries an ISO insert code: CNMG 12 04 08.

16
00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:06.865
You read it from left to right.

17
00:01:07.765 --> 00:01:11.248
C is the shape: a diamond with 80 degree corners.

18
00:01:11.798 --> 00:01:16.108
N: no clearance angle, so both faces can be used.

19
00:01:16.658 --> 00:01:22.259
M is the tolerance, and G: a hole for clamping, and chip breakers.

20
00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:28.070
12 is the size: an inscribed circle of 12.7 millimetres.

21
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.753
04 is the thickness: 4.76 millimetres.

22
00:01:33.303 --> 00:01:37.959
And 08 is the nose radius: 0.8 millimetres.

23
00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:42.786
Two corners per face, two faces: 4 edges.

24
00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:50.713
At €14 an insert, that is 3 euros 50 an edge, and at 70 parts an edge,

25
00:01:50.913 --> 00:01:52.387
5 cents a part.

26
00:01:53.587 --> 00:01:57.140
The box also names the grade: the carbide and the coating,

27
00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.430
chosen for a material group.

28
00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:07.316
P blue is steel, M yellow stainless steel, K red cast iron, N green aluminium,

29
00:02:07.515 --> 00:02:13.010
S brown titanium and heat-resistant alloys, and H grey hardened steel.

30
00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:17.675
A P grade in stainless steel works, but badly.

31
00:02:18.224 --> 00:02:20.569
Does the grade on the box match this job?

32
00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:23.255
Every edge wears.

33
00:02:23.805 --> 00:02:29.288
With flank wear, a dull band grows along the edge: slowly at first,

34
00:02:29.488 --> 00:02:31.450
then faster and faster.

35
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:38.355
On aluminium and soft steel, material welds onto the edge: a built-up edge.

36
00:02:38.905 --> 00:02:41.517
That spoils the size and the surface.

37
00:02:42.417 --> 00:02:45.331
You see wear in drifting sizes and burrs.

38
00:02:45.881 --> 00:02:49.573
And the spindle load rises: the motor has to work harder.

39
00:02:50.773 --> 00:02:55.823
Emulsion cools, lubricates and flushes the chips away: oil in water,

40
00:02:56.023 --> 00:02:58.473
usually 5 to 8 percent oil.

41
00:02:59.373 --> 00:03:03.750
With presetting you measure the length and radius of a tool outside the machine.

42
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.065
So the machine keeps cutting.

43
00:03:06.965 --> 00:03:10.982
At the limit a sister tool takes over: an identical spare tool.

44
00:03:11.532 --> 00:03:19.719
An insert of €12 with 4 edges is €3 an edge: at 80 parts, 3.75 cents a part.

45
00:03:20.919 --> 00:03:25.472
The Taylor equation from 1907 links tool life to speed:

46
00:03:25.472 --> 00:03:28.535
V times T to the power n is constant.

47
00:03:29.435 --> 00:03:33.998
The Taylor exponent n is often about 0.25 for carbide.

48
00:03:34.548 --> 00:03:43.238
Then 25 percent faster, from 200 to 250, leaves only 0.41 of the tool life.

49
00:03:44.138 --> 00:03:49.200
At 200 metres a minute an edge lasts 15 minutes: 30 parts.

50
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:55.604
At 220 that becomes 10.2 minutes, and about 22 parts.

51
00:03:56.504 --> 00:03:58.176
In a tool-life test,

52
00:03:58.376 --> 00:04:03.984
the wear criterion is usually a wear land of 0.3 millimetres on average.

53
00:04:04.884 --> 00:04:07.845
And tool monitoring watches the spindle load,

54
00:04:08.045 --> 00:04:10.657
and stops the machine when a tool breaks.

55
00:04:11.857 --> 00:04:15.889
A part costs machine time, tool changes and tools.

56
00:04:16.438 --> 00:04:20.493
Cutting faster shrinks the machine time, but grows the rest.

57
00:04:21.393 --> 00:04:23.529
The change time is 2 minutes.

58
00:04:24.079 --> 00:04:30.958
An edge of €4, at 1 euro 50 a minute, is 2.67 machine minutes.

59
00:04:31.508 --> 00:04:34.143
Together, 4.67 minutes.

60
00:04:35.043 --> 00:04:42.778
With n at 0.25, the economic tool life is 3 times 4.67: 14 minutes.

61
00:04:43.678 --> 00:04:48.624
That gives the economic cutting speed: about 204 metres a minute,

62
00:04:48.824 --> 00:04:50.647
the lowest point of the cost.

63
00:04:51.547 --> 00:04:57.355
With a sister tool, the stop drops to 0.3 minutes: 8.9 minutes,

64
00:04:57.555 --> 00:05:00.411
at 228 metres a minute.

65
00:05:00.961 --> 00:05:03.805
Faster only pays when changing is cheap.

66
00:05:05.005 --> 00:05:09.103
The bigger money is often in tool standardisation: fewer kinds,

67
00:05:09.303 --> 00:05:13.706
the same on every machine, and cutting data everyone knows.

68
00:05:14.606 --> 00:05:17.137
So don’t chase the cheapest insert.

69
00:05:17.687 --> 00:05:19.463
Chase the cheapest part.
